+# Weigh a new service against Postgres
+
+For whoever proposes adding a service to larder. A new service is added only if it does something Postgres cannot,
+and at least two volunteers can run and restore it, because the rule has had to be rediscovered three times in 2025.
+
+## Context
+
+Larder runs one database and one web app ([architecture](../architecture.md)), kept on purpose to a size its
+volunteers can operate. Three proposals have tested that on the same two forces: a proposed service must do something
+Postgres cannot, and two volunteers must be able to restore it.
+
+- **2023, Redis for sessions**: rejected before it shipped, because only one volunteer would have run it
+  ([keep sessions in the database](0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md)).
+- **March 2025, Redis for Celery's job queue**: shipped, then evicted 300 queued receipt jobs on a Saturday night;
+  one volunteer could restore it, and spent a day resending receipts by hand
+  ([run background jobs on Postgres](0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md)).
+- **October 2025, Elasticsearch for pantry item search**: proposed to fix a four-second search that missed "Rice,
+  brown"; dropped once Postgres full-text search on the item name did the same in 40ms (`notes/issue-70.md`).
+
+Each time, Postgres already did the job, or could be made to.
+
+## Decision
+
+Before proposing a service beyond larder's one database and one web app, state in the proposal:
+
+1. What the service does that Postgres cannot.
+2. Which two volunteers can run it and restore it without the person who set it up.
+
+If either answer is missing, the proposal is not ready, regardless of how well the service fits the feature.
+
+This is not a ban on outside services: a hosted service that nobody on the team has to run, such as the card
+gateway, is a different trade and is not covered by this rule.
+
+## Consequences
+
+A proposal to add a service starts from these two questions instead of rediscovering them. Sessions and background
+jobs stay on Postgres because neither answer was met; the card gateway stays outside because the rule does not apply
+to it.
